  "statement": "Strict liability outperforms negligence precisely where AI harm is severe, foreseeable as a class but not as an instance, and where design control sits upstream of the victim. Negligence asks the injured party to reconstruct fault inside a system whose causal chain is deliberately fragmented across developer, deployer, and user. No individual claimant can meet that forensic burden. The cancer patient misdiagnosed by a clinical AI cannot subpoena the training data, audit the deployment configuration, and prove which actor breached a duty of care. Pharmaceutical and aviation product liability abandoned that standard for exactly this reason — the injured party cannot audit the design that injured them.\n\nA negligence regime is structurally blind, and that blindness is its central failure. It deters only the harms that get litigated. The cheap-to-prove failure — the car that swerves on a clear road with a single identifiable victim — reaches a courtroom. The diffuse failure — the automated lending model that quietly denies credit to ten thousand applicants across an unprovable statistical pattern — never does. Strict liability paired with mandatory insurance prices the full expected loss before deployment, not after a winnable case emerges. It forces safety into the design phase rather than waiting for the litigation that statistically never comes for the systemic harms.\n\nThe strongest version of the opposing case deserves a fair hearing: strict liability could over-deter, taxing beneficial AI with the cost of speculative harm and freezing deployment by firms that would have operated safely. That insight is real, and it identifies the genuine risk of any liability regime — that you punish the careful alongside the reckless. Where I part ways is on what the deterred deployment actually represents. A developer who cannot underwrite their own deployment risk is not being chilled. They are being told, correctly, that the risk is not yet bearable at scale.